h1: Moving a solar business off spreadsheets, in a sensible order.
lead: The switch fails when it starts with software and succeeds when it starts with the business. This is the order that keeps the team on side and the data clean.
answer: Study how jobs flow today, clean the data worth keeping, define stages and roles, load open jobs, train each desk on its own work, and switch over by job, not by department.
published: 2026-10-11
sections:
  - h: Study how jobs flow today
    p:
      - Write down the path a job takes, from first call to final payment and service. Note where it waits, who hands it over and which paper or sheet it lives on.
      - This map becomes the delivery checklist. Systems fail when they force a flow nobody follows, so start from what really happens.
  - h: Decide what data is worth keeping
    p:
      - Old sheets hold duplicates, half-finished entries and customers who will never buy. Keep open jobs, active customers, the material catalogue and live AMCs. Archive the rest.
  - h: Define stages, documents and owners
    p:
      - For each type of job, list the stages, the documents each needs and who owns it. Include utility and subsidy steps where they apply, and decide which can be skipped for a given job.
  - h: Define roles
    p:
      - List the seats in your company and what each should see and do. Define who approves quotations, who verifies claims and who exports data.
  - h: Load the open jobs
    p:
      - Load customers, the catalogue and jobs that are currently open, with their stage and pending balance. Starting with live work gives the team a reason to use the system on day one.
  - h: Train each desk on its own work
    p:
      - Sales needs the pipeline, surveyors need the phone survey, the store needs stock movements, and accounts needs claims. Teaching each group only its own screens keeps training short.
  - h: Switch over by job
    p:
      - Pick a date after which every new enquiry goes into the system. Let jobs already in flight finish where they are, or move them across at a natural handover point.
  - h: Review after two weeks
    p:
      - Ask each desk what is slowing them. Adjust stages, roles and fields, and keep what works. A good system is configured to the business, so small changes in the first weeks are normal.
takeaways:
  - Start from the real job flow, not from the software.
  - Load live work, not history.
  - Train by desk and switch over by job.
